WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.761
 

00:00:04.761 --> 00:00:07.010
CHARLES NESSON: You can't tell the
story of the American jury

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:09.990
without telling the story of America.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:14.030
It starts with the
Declaration of Independence

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:21.310
in which we, as a legal matter, define
ourselves as an independent people

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:26.190
and assert our sovereignty as a people.

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:31.210
That is the Declaration of Independence.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:37.160
We don't get to the jury until we
get to the Constitution of the state.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:45.630
In the Constitution of the state, the
jury is the method that we the people

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:51.250
adopted as our strategy
for controlling the state.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:56.270
The most fundamental relationship
between citizen and state

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:59.020
is defined by the jury.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:01.710
The jury took two forms.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:07.580
The core idea is one of justice.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:12.940
And the core idea of
justice is fair trial.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:18.460
And the fair trial has
three parts-- a fair charge,

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:25.410
a fair opportunity to
defend, and a fair verdict.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:31.070
The jury is the linchpin of fair trial.

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:36.520
First, with respect to the
charge, the grand jury.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:41.000
The idea was we the
people should control

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:45.920
what charge can be brought
against a person that

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:51.400
can put him in such jeopardy
before a jury of his peers

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:54.920
that they could take his freedom away.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:56.840
That's the grand jury.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:01.210
And then, the opportunity
to defend, that

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:06.480
becomes cross-examination,
the high points of the trial,

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:12.620
the actual testing of the witnesses
who have made the charge, and then

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:13.960
the verdict.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:16.810
And that's the petit jury.

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:22.170
The idea of 12 coming
to a unanimous verdict.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:25.080
And that's the idea of the jury.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:32.250
And the thought is that if the state
couldn't take a man's freedom away

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:42.290
unless he had a fair trial, the
jury was the bulwark of freedom.

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:45.160
That's the story of the jury.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:50.539
 

